#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# curry_applied

#% text
# `curry_applied(f, spec)` is `f` taking its arguments over several calls: `curry_applied(f, n)(a1)(a2)...(an)` is `f(a1, ..., an)`; `curry_applied(spec)(f)` is `curry_applied(f, spec)`. The spec is `n`, `[i1, ..., ik]` (`f` of the arguments `i1`, ..., `ik` of `max(i)` arguments) or `n -> [i1, ..., ik]`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
curry_applied(f, 3)(a)(b)(c)
#% output
#= f(a, b, c)

#% cell
curry_applied(2)(f)(x)(y)
#% output
#= f(x, y)

#% cell
map(curry_applied(add, 2)(10), [1, 2, 3])
#% output
#= [11, 12, 13]

#% cell
curry_applied(integrate, [3, 1, 2])(x)(y)(x*sin(y))
#% output
#= (-1/2)*cos(y)*x^2

#% cell
curry_applied(f, 4 -> [2, 3])(a)(b)(c)(d)
#% output
#= f(b, c)

#% cell
curry_applied(f, 3)(a, b)
#% output
#= curry_applied(f, 3)(a, b)

#% cell
curry_applied(composition, 3)(f, g, h, x)
#% output
#= f(g(h(x)))

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `operator_applied`, `reverse_applied`
